Bellman’s Optimality Principle: 

For a policy to be truly optimal, it must have the property of every decision from the initial state must also be optimal. Regardless of how you break up the route of the optimal path, each individual substructure must be optimal in it of itself.  

 

Control Theory: 

Focuses on dynamic systems and how to choose inputs that will satisfy the desired objective. Usually involves observing the state and establishing controllers with the intention of creating stability and optimality.  

 

Open Loop: 

The control behind an action is pre-established and independent from the product of the action. Having the control set beforehand prevents the system from being adaptive and prone to errors in unexpected scenarios.  

 

Closed Loop (Feedback): 

The control behind an action is dependent on its state/output. Like a heating system turning off/on depending on the temperature of the area, it is heating. Tends to be the preferred option, especially in dynamic programming.  

 

Stability: 

We could consider a system stable if small disturbance only results in a small deviation and does not get out of control. In a better case, it would be asymptotically stable, which means that even with a small disturbance, the system will return back to its baseline. In this scenario, the system should always end up back at equilibrium.  

 

(2/4/26-2/11/26) 
Controllability: 

The ability to choose an input that can cause a desired change in the state. If no such inputs can reliably produce an intended state, then it would be considered not controllable. Lack of controllability does not necessarily affect the stability; the dynamics can still be stable; known as stabilizable. 

Observability: 

The ability to use output measurements to view the state of the system. If it is not observable, then the controller will have no hope of being able to stabilize the system. 

 

Mini max and alpha beta pruning  

Alpha Beta Pruning – keeps track of two scores alpha (best maximizer score) and beta (best minimizer score) and stops exploring a branch if its best possible outcome is no better than a previously found option. This is assuming the opponent plays the best moves, and if their advantage is better or they will win then disregard that path. 

 

Dynamic Programming: 

Starts with the simple idea of storing solutions to subproblems instead of recursively solving every problem each time it comes up. This frees up a lot of time from wasteful and unnecessary computation, potentially reducing exponential time complexities down to polynomial. “Remembering the past to solve the future faster” (Geeks). There are two main ways to store the results of these subproblems: memoization (top down) or tabulation (bottom up).  

 

Optimal Substructure: 

Using the optimal results to subproblems to gurantee the optimal solution to the total problem. 

 

Overlapping Subproblems: 

Especially in recursive problems, though not exclusively, we will see the same problem come up repeatedly. Solving these problems each individually should always be avoided. 

 

Memoization: 

Keeps the problem recursive but simply uses a table to store the data as it goes. So, before any recursive calls, it will check the table for a pre-established solution. If there is no solution, then it will go about its normal operations and then store the solution it generates to the table to prevent any repeat calculations.  

 

Tabulation: 

This follows the same idea as memoization but instead drops the recursive approach for an iterative one. It starts at the smallest subproblem and works its way up to the final solution to the problem. Storing its results in a table all the way up which should simplify the calculations in the later subproblems.  

