Continuous actions in Control Theory: 

Continuous Action - an input into the system for every moment in time. Different from a normal set list of input options, now we are choosing a real number to input continuously through time to represent our change to the system.  

Since we are not simply choosing a single action, we must design a control law for the continuous action 

 

Advantages of Continuous Time Control Systems: 

Since it is continuous, they inherently can react quicker to changes from input signals. This quicker reaction time makes tolerable to disruptions and loud surroundings 

Offers higher levels of accuracy and precision with operations 

Integrate easily to real life systems such as analog sensors and actuators 

 

Disadvantages of Continuous Time Control Systems: 

Continuous time system analysis implements differential equations which can be more time-consuming than discrete time systems 

May need to implement filtering out or reduction of analog signal noise. This noise can very much negatively impact the performance of our system.  

 

